;; Docs for text/json.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "json.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "JSON, read into a tree: RFC 8259, one character at a time.")
  (see Json parse-json read-json)
  (example """
    (import (text json))

    (match (parse-json "{\"name\": \"http\", \"tags\": [\"net\", \"web\"]}")
      ((Ok (JsonObj m)) (map-try-ref m "name"))   ; (Some (JsonStr "http"))
      ((Ok other) None)
      ((Err why) (println why) None))
    """)
  (reference """
    A document is read into a @code{Json}, a union with a case for each
    kind of JSON value. Reading a record straight from JSON, and writing one
    as JSON, is @code{(text json-codec)}'s: its @code{def/json-type} declares
    a record and the instances that convert it.

    @strong{What is read.} Exactly RFC 8259: one value, with whitespace
    around it and nothing else after it. A byte order mark before the value is
    skipped. There are no comments, no trailing commas, no single quotes and
    no unquoted keys. A number may not have a leading zero, and needs digits
    after its decimal point and its exponent. A control character in a string
    has to be escaped, and a @code{\u} escape for a surrogate has to be one
    half of a pair of them; the pair is read as one character. The port is
    read as UTF-8, and bytes that are not UTF-8 read as U+FFFD, as they do
    on any port.

    @strong{Numbers.} A number without a fraction or an exponent is a
    @code{JsonInt}, a @code{long}. One with either, or one too wide for a
    @code{long}, is a @code{JsonFloat}. So @code{1} and @code{1.0} are
    different values in the tree; @code{(text json-codec)} reads either into
    a @code{double}, and only the first into an integer.

    @strong{Objects.} An object is a @code{(Map string Json)}, so the order
    its keys were written in is not kept, and when a key is written twice the
    last value wins.

    @strong{Errors.} A document that is not JSON is an @code{Err} saying
    where and why: @code{line 1, column 7: ']' does not begin a value}. A
    column is a character, so one outside the BMP is one column. Nothing is
    raised. A document nested deeper than
    @code{json-max-depth} is refused the same way rather than exhausting the
    stack.

    @strong{Not here yet.} There is no writer: a @code{Json} cannot yet be
    turned back into text by this module.
    """))

(:doc Json
  (summary "A JSON value.")
  (case JsonNull "null.")
  (case JsonBool "true or false.")
  (case JsonInt "A number with no fraction and no exponent that fits in a long.")
  (case JsonFloat "Any other number.")
  (case JsonStr "A string, its escapes resolved.")
  (case JsonArr "An array, in order.")
  (case JsonObj "An object, by key. The order of the keys is not kept; a key written twice keeps its last value.")
  (see parse-json))

(:doc json-max-depth
  (summary "How deep arrays and objects may nest before reading refuses the document: 1000.")
  (see read-json))

(:doc read-json
  (summary "Reads one JSON document from a port.")
  (arg port "The port. Everything left in it is read: one value, and only space after it.")
  (returns "The value, or why the text is not JSON, with its line and column.")
  (see parse-json)
  (reference """
    A byte order mark before the value is skipped and does not count as a
    column. On a bjoroutine the port is read without blocking the thread.
    """))

(:doc parse-json
  (summary "Reads one JSON document from a string.")
  (arg text "The document.")
  (returns "The value, or why the text is not JSON, with its line and column.")
  (see read-json)
  (example """
    (parse-json "[1, 2.5, null]")   ; (Ok (JsonArr [(JsonInt 1) (JsonFloat 2.5) JsonNull]))
    (parse-json "[1, 2,]")          ; (Err "line 1, column 7: ']' does not begin a value")
    """))
